# Zonal -> Nodal 4 Zone Testing

This directory contains a simple test case for the zonal to nodal aggregation. The original system has 4 buses with a generator on bus 1 and bus 3, loads on bus 2 and bus 4, and with lines between bus1/bus2, bus1/bus3, bus1/bus4, bus3/bus4. The "zonal" approach places buses 1 and 2 into one zone and buses 3 and 4 into another zone. 

The `mapping_function.jl` file goes through the original data and creates new sets of CSVs which can be loaded directly into GenX. For this problem, it creates a new subdirectory called `aggregated` for the zonal problem and it creates `zone1` and `zone2` which represent the nodal problems. You can see at the end of this file how the functions can be called and it includes some mapping dictionaries (that assign each bus to a zone). Note that "zone" is a little bit confusing because GenX calls everything a "zone." In our case, our system is made up of buses/nodes that are placed into higher level zones, but each bus/node is called a "zone" by GenX. 

After the `mapping_function.jl` is run, you can see in the `run_downscaling.jl` file that the aggregated file can be called and run and then each zone can be called and run. I will note I did make some manual changes to the nodal data (zone1 and zone2) before solving. The solution of the aggregated problem ships power from the first zone to the second at all time points, so I added a demand to bus1 in zone 1 of the nodal problem at each time point that represented the power export. Then, because there was power being exported to buses 3 and 4 in zone2, I added two thermal generators in each of these buses which I then fix to specific production values. 

Finally, I'll note I ran this with the PTDF_formulation branch from Mike's GenX fork. This had a small change to the `case_runners.jl` in the source code which allowed me to pull the model directly from the case runner, so you may notice that the case runner call is returning a JuMP model. Happy to share more about this if you want more details. 